  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Shower and thunderstorm chances shift southward\nwith a cold front through Friday, which will be followed by \ncooler temperatures.\n\nHeight falls amidst a humid airmass resulted in showers and\nthunderstorms expanding in coverage from northwest to southeast\nover the past several hours. Poor mid level lapse rates and weak\nshear have prevented the convection from becoming too\ndeep/strong. The weak shear and sufficient (but generally modest)\nDCAPE has allow the storms to become outflow dominant, with \nsome observed gusts of 30-40 mph. At this point the probability \nof severe thunderstorms is low. Instability decreases east of \nthe I-95 corridor, and so far, convection has been weakening as \nit approaches this threshold. The main question now is whether\nthere is another round of showers later this afternoon and\nevening closer to the approach cold front. This would likely\noriginate from the activity in the upper Ohio Valley to northern\nPennsylvania. Given the diurnal timing and an airmass which has\nalready been cooled from the previous convection and lingering\nclouds, thunder appears less likely with this round.\n\nWhile the cold front drops in later tonight, guidance indicates\nsome patchy fog could develop before the wind shift occurs. This\nwould most likely occur along rivers and low lying areas,\nalthough low clouds may move into the Allegheny and Potomac\nHighlands which could also reduce visibility.\n\nWhile the wind shift portion of the cold front likely pushes\nsouth of the area Friday morning, the moisture gradient may hang\nback across central Virginia up to the Potomac Highlands. As a \nshortwave trough swings through during the afternoon, some \nadditional showers and thunderstorms may develop in these areas.\nTemperatures will fall back into the lower to mid 80s with\nnortherly gusts up to 20 mph at times. There may also be a fair\namount of stratocumulus at times as well. A cooler night lies \nahead as high pressure crosses southern Quebec into New England.\nFriday night's lows should be in the upper 50s to mid 60s. \n\nHigh pressure to the northeast on Saturday should provide enough\ndry air to keep rain at bay. One exception may be along the\nAllegheny Front due to convergence in an inverted surface\ntrough. Warm advection aloft and mid level perturbations will \nlikely provide cloud cover areawide. Temperatures will be \ncloser to climatology, ranging from the mid 70s to lower 80s.\nLow pressure moving into the Ohio Valley may increase rain\nchances Saturday night.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms return at times Sunday\ninto next week, with temperatures likely falling below normal.\n\nA cold front drops through the forecast area on Sunday before \nstalling to the south, bringing daily chances for showers and \nthunderstorms. In addition to daily precipitation, a cooler airmass \nbuilds overhead in the wake of the cold front, yielding cooling \ntemperatures each day. On Sunday, high temperatures will be in the \nlow 80s for most (70s mtns) with overnight lows in the 60s. By \nmidweek, high temperatures will top out in the upper 50s to 60s with \novernight lows in the 50s. These temperatures remain somewhat\nuncertain with large model spread, but the forecast favors the\ncooler side of guidance given a cold air damming surface\npattern. Overall, nearby disturbances bring unsettled weather \nto the region beginning Sunday and persisting through midweek.",
